class:: PerlinNoise3D
summary:: Periodic 3D Perlin-noise/fBm path oscillator
categories:: UGens>Generators
related:: Classes/PerlinNoise, Classes/PerlinNoise2D, Guides/PerlinNoisePorts, Classes/LFNoise2, Classes/LeakDC

description::

PerlinNoise3D generates one audio signal by scanning a periodic three-dimensional gradient-noise volume. One fast axis and two slow axes make a pitched texture whose waveform evolves as the path moves between slices. Negative speeds scan backwards. Three-dimensional refers to the noise coordinates, not to spatial audio or the number of output channels.

This extends this project's periodic oscillator approach, inspired by Artem Popov's paper link::https://lac.linuxaudio.org/2018/pages/event/14/::, to a 3D field. The corner-gradient construction follows the technique in Ken Perlin's link::https://cs.nyu.edu/~perlin/noise/::. It is a standalone implementation with seeded coordinate hashing and octave-dependent wrapping, not a sample-for-sample copy of either upstream implementation.

For each octave, the DSP hashes the eight surrounding lattice corners to choose among twelve unit-length cube-edge gradients, takes their dot products with the corner-to-point offsets, and interpolates along x, y, and z using the quintic fade curve. The hash wraps independently in all three dimensions, making the field seamless across each normalized phase boundary. Octaves double the lattice density (lacunarity 2), use distinct derived seeds, and decrease in amplitude according to persistence. The sum is divided by its amplitude sum, multiplied by 1.6, and clipped to -1..1 before mul/add.

A fixed slice of this volume differs from link::Classes/PerlinNoise2D:: because the gradient construction and corner contributions differ. A periodic volume also need not give a short repeating trajectory when its axis speeds have unrelated ratios. Fixed slices can contain DC; use link::Classes/LeakDC:: for audible oscillator patches. A completely stationary path outputs a constant value, which is zero at lattice vertices. Some single-octave slices can also be silent.

The closest vanilla comparison is link::Classes/LFNoise2:: for smooth noise modulation. PerlinNoise3D instead follows a repeatable path through a seeded periodic volume and combines multiple scales of detail; it does not generate random breakpoint values over time.

subsection:: Limits and modulation

There is no oversampling or band limiting. High speeds, many octaves, and high persistence increase aliasing; reducing octaves or persistence reduces the high-frequency content. The largest axis speed multiplied by code::2 ** (octaves - 1):: estimates the highest lattice traversal rate, but is not a guarantee of alias-free output. Each octave evaluates eight corners, so high octave counts and multichannel expansion increase CPU use.

Seed and persistence changes take effect immediately and may click. Octave changes wait until every moving axis wraps in the same sample; stationary axes are ignored. With only one moving axis this is its next wrap. With unrelated speeds or offsets a shared wrap may be rare or never occur, and with all speeds zero a pending change remains pending. Such a boundary is not necessarily a zero crossing on a fixed slice, so octave changes are not guaranteed click-free. Keep octaves fixed for continuously drifting paths, or crossfade separate synths for smooth structural changes.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: xfreq
X-axis phase speed in cycles per second, clipped to code::-SampleRate * 0.499..SampleRate * 0.499::. Supports audio-rate modulation; non-finite values become zero.

argument:: yfreq
Y-axis phase speed, with the same range and rate support as xfreq. Zero holds the initial y slice.

argument:: zfreq
Z-axis phase speed, with the same range and rate support as xfreq. Zero holds the initial z slice.

argument:: xphase
Initial normalized x coordinate, wrapped to 0..1 and read only at synth creation. Non-finite values become zero.

argument:: yphase
Initial normalized y coordinate, with the same wrapping and initialization-only behavior as xphase.

argument:: zphase
Initial normalized z coordinate, with the same wrapping and initialization-only behavior as xphase.

argument:: octaves
Number of fBm octaves, rounded to the nearest integer and clipped to 1..32. Supports audio-rate input, subject to the shared-wrap latch described above. Non-finite values become 1.

argument:: persistence
Per-octave amplitude multiplier, clipped to 0..1. Zero leaves only the first octave; 1 gives equal octave weights. Supports audio-rate modulation; non-finite values become 0.5.

argument:: seed
Deterministic gradient seed, rounded with code::floor(seed + 0.5):: and wrapped modulo 2 ** 32. Supports audio-rate input; non-finite values become zero. Use small integer seeds for reliable float precision.

argument:: mul
Output multiplier.

argument:: add
Output offset.

examples::

code::
// A pitched scan with two slowly moving slice coordinates.
(
{
	var sig = PerlinNoise3D.ar(110, 0.17, 0.11,
		xphase: 0.1, yphase: 0.35, zphase: 0.6,
		octaves: 6, persistence: 0.5, seed: 12);
	var env = EnvGen.kr(Env.linen(0.02, 8, 0.2), doneAction: 2);
	(LeakDC.ar(sig) * env * 0.15) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
// Two fixed slices form a stereo pair; negative z speed scans backwards.
(
{
	var sig = PerlinNoise3D.ar(0, 0, -90,
		xphase: [0.23, 0.61], yphase: 0.4, zphase: 0.2,
		octaves: 6, persistence: 0.55, seed: 1001);
	var env = EnvGen.kr(Env.linen(0.02, 8, 0.2), doneAction: 2);
	LeakDC.ar(sig) * env * 0.15
}.play;
)
::

code::
// Slow 3D motion as a deterministic pitch modulator.
(
{
	var motion = PerlinNoise3D.ar(0.3, 0.17, -0.11,
		xphase: 0.1, yphase: 0.4, zphase: 0.7,
		octaves: 4, persistence: 0.5, seed: 42);
	var env = EnvGen.kr(Env.linen(0.02, 10, 0.2), doneAction: 2);
	(SinOsc.ar(motion.linexp(-1, 1, 110, 880)) * env * 0.1) ! 2
}.play;
)
::
